        content: "Marie Curie died at **5:00 a.m. on July 4, 1934**, at the **Sancellemoz sanatorium** in **Passy**, in the French Alps, two months short of her 67th birthday. The diagnosis given on her death certificate was **\"aplastic pernicious anemia of rapid, feverish development. The bone marrow did not react, probably because it had been injured by a long accumulation of radiation.\"** The signing physician was **Dr. Tobé**, the sanatorium's director, who had watched her hemoglobin and white-blood-cell counts drop steadily over the preceding six weeks.\n\nAplastic anemia is a failure of the **bone marrow** to produce new red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets. In 1934 no treatment existed; bone-marrow transplantation would not become possible until the 1960s. Patients died of **hemorrhage** (no platelets) or **infection** (no white cells), usually within months of diagnosis. Marie's daughter Ève recorded her mother's final delirium as a continued counting of fevers and a worried muttering about a misplaced laboratory thermometer.\n\nThe coffin was sealed in **lead** at the family's request. Fifty years later, when she and Pierre were exhumed for reburial in the **Panthéon** in **April 1995**, **Geiger counters at the gravesite registered above-background but below dangerous levels** — most of the radium had been excreted; what remained had decayed slightly; the contamination feared by the Sancellemoz staff in 1934 had been overestimated. But the bone marrow had not been overestimated. It was destroyed.",
